{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-331913" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation \n\n$$\n2\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+2y=-{x}^{2}-2x+4.\n$$\n\nFind the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ to this equation." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040400/state.json" "file_sha256" "00c2f57a1e14ce294f3828d1cea1966c3408c0d0f3ac5366645345a6a40072e1"} {"question" "q-331913" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "The right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular solution is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $ and $\\gamma ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n2(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+2(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}-2x+4\n$$\n\nGrouping the terms on the left-hand side gives\n\n$$\n2\\alpha {x}^{2}+(4\\alpha +2\\beta )x+(2\\beta +2\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}-2x+4.\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}2\\alpha =-1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 4\\alpha +2\\beta =-2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ 2\\beta +2\\gamma =4\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-\\frac{1}{2},\\beta =0,\\gamma =2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular integral ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040400/state.json" "file_sha256" "00c2f57a1e14ce294f3828d1cea1966c3408c0d0f3ac5366645345a6a40072e1"} {"question" "q-331913" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040400/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8c7a8ddd558282989143d3a80d5d2f442156b5a3285ad5a583c66ebbc09d55c4"} {"question" "q-331913" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040400/state.json" "file_sha256" "00c2f57a1e14ce294f3828d1cea1966c3408c0d0f3ac5366645345a6a40072e1"} {"question" "q-331913" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "-2{x}^{2}"] ["math" "-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}"] ["math" "-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2"] ["math" "\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}-2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040400/state.json" "file_sha256" "00c2f57a1e14ce294f3828d1cea1966c3408c0d0f3ac5366645345a6a40072e1"} {"question" "q-331913" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}+2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040400/state.json" "file_sha256" "00c2f57a1e14ce294f3828d1cea1966c3408c0d0f3ac5366645345a6a40072e1"} {"question" "q-332550" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=-{x}^{2}+2x-3.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040400/state.json" "file_sha256" "00c2f57a1e14ce294f3828d1cea1966c3408c0d0f3ac5366645345a6a40072e1"} {"question" "q-332550" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}+{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $, and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$  we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}+2x-3\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\alpha {x}^{2}+(2\\alpha +\\beta )x+(\\beta +\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}+2x-3\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}\\alpha =-1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 2\\alpha +\\beta =2\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\beta +\\gamma =-3\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-1,\\beta =4,\\gamma =-7.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-{x}^{2}+4x-7.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-x}-{x}^{2}+4x-7.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040400/state.json" "file_sha256" "00c2f57a1e14ce294f3828d1cea1966c3408c0d0f3ac5366645345a6a40072e1"} {"question" "q-332550" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040400/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8c7a8ddd558282989143d3a80d5d2f442156b5a3285ad5a583c66ebbc09d55c4"} {"question" "q-332550" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040400/state.json" "file_sha256" "00c2f57a1e14ce294f3828d1cea1966c3408c0d0f3ac5366645345a6a40072e1"} {"question" "q-332550" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=A{e}^{-x}+{x}^{2}-4x-7"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-x}-3{x}^{2}+4x-7"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-x}-{x}^{2}+2x-7"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-x}-{x}^{2}+4x+7"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-x}-{x}^{2}+4x-7"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040400/state.json" "file_sha256" "00c2f57a1e14ce294f3828d1cea1966c3408c0d0f3ac5366645345a6a40072e1"} {"question" "q-332550" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=A{e}^{-x}-{x}^{2}+4x-7"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040400/state.json" "file_sha256" "00c2f57a1e14ce294f3828d1cea1966c3408c0d0f3ac5366645345a6a40072e1"} {"question" "q-332552" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the differential equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}+y=-{x}^{2}-x-1.\n$$\n\nFind the general solution to this equation given that the complementary solution is ${y}_{c}(x)=A{e}^{-x},$ where $A$ is an arbitrary constant." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040400/state.json" "file_sha256" "00c2f57a1e14ce294f3828d1cea1966c3408c0d0f3ac5366645345a6a40072e1"} {"question" "q-332552" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{c}}{\\text{d}x}+{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $2.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $2,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta $, and $\\gamma $ are constants that are to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=2\\alpha x+\\beta .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ $\\beta ,$ and $\\gamma ,$  we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n(2\\alpha x+\\beta )+(\\alpha {x}^{2}+\\beta x+\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}-x-1\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\alpha {x}^{2}+(2\\alpha +\\beta )x+(\\beta +\\gamma )=-{x}^{2}-x-1\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}\\alpha =-1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,{x}^{2}) \\\\ 2\\alpha +\\beta =-1\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\beta +\\gamma =-1\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-1,\\beta =1,\\gamma =-2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-{x}^{2}+x-2.\n$$\n\nNow, since the general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny={y}_{c}+{y}_{p}\n$$\n\nthe general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-x}-{x}^{2}+x-2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040400/state.json" "file_sha256" "00c2f57a1e14ce294f3828d1cea1966c3408c0d0f3ac5366645345a6a40072e1"} {"question" "q-332552" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/hard "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040400/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8c7a8ddd558282989143d3a80d5d2f442156b5a3285ad5a583c66ebbc09d55c4"} {"question" "q-332552" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040400/state.json" "file_sha256" "00c2f57a1e14ce294f3828d1cea1966c3408c0d0f3ac5366645345a6a40072e1"} {"question" "q-332552" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "y=A{e}^{-x}+{x}^{2}-x-2"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-x}-2{x}^{2}+x-2"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-x}-{x}^{2}+x+2"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-x}-{x}^{2}+x-2"] ["math" "y=A{e}^{-x}-{x}^{2}-x-2"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040400/state.json" "file_sha256" "00c2f57a1e14ce294f3828d1cea1966c3408c0d0f3ac5366645345a6a40072e1"} {"question" "q-332552" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "y=A{e}^{-x}-{x}^{2}+x-2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040400/state.json" "file_sha256" "00c2f57a1e14ce294f3828d1cea1966c3408c0d0f3ac5366645345a6a40072e1"} {"question" "q-332756" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+3y=-6x+1,\\,y(0)=\\frac{5}{3}\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+3y=-6x+1\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040400/state.json" "file_sha256" "00c2f57a1e14ce294f3828d1cea1966c3408c0d0f3ac5366645345a6a40072e1"} {"question" "q-332756" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+3{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-3x}\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a polynomial of degree $1.$ Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a polynomial of degree $1,$ i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\alpha x+\\beta \n$$\n\nwhere $\\alpha $ and $\\beta $ are constants to be determined. \n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=\\alpha .\n$$\n\nTo find the values of $\\alpha ,$ and $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n\\alpha +3(\\alpha x+\\beta )=-6x+1\n$$\n\nWe then group the terms on the left-hand side, as follows:\n\n$$\n3\\alpha x+(\\alpha +3\\beta )=-6x+1\n$$\n\nEquating the coefficients, we get the following system of equations:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\begin{aligned}3\\alpha =-6\\, & (\\text{equating the coefficients of}\\,\\,x) \\\\ \\alpha +3\\beta =1\\, & (\\text{equating the constants})\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving this system gives $\\alpha =-2,\\,\\beta =1.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=-2x+1.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-3x}-2x+1.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=\\frac{5}{3}$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{-3(0)}-2(0)+1 & =\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ A+1 & =\\frac{5}{3} \\\\ A & =\\frac{2}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=\\frac{2}{3}{e}^{-3x}-2x+1.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040400/state.json" "file_sha256" "00c2f57a1e14ce294f3828d1cea1966c3408c0d0f3ac5366645345a6a40072e1"} {"question" "q-332756" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040400/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8c7a8ddd558282989143d3a80d5d2f442156b5a3285ad5a583c66ebbc09d55c4"} {"question" "q-332756" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040400/state.json" "file_sha256" "00c2f57a1e14ce294f3828d1cea1966c3408c0d0f3ac5366645345a6a40072e1"} {"question" "q-332756" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "\\frac{2}{3}e^{-3x}-2x+1"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040400/state.json" "file_sha256" "00c2f57a1e14ce294f3828d1cea1966c3408c0d0f3ac5366645345a6a40072e1"} {"question" "q-332782" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Consider the following initial value problem:\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+5y=10,\\,y(0)=1\n$$\n\nGiven that the differential equation \n\n$$\n{y}^{′}+5y=10\n$$\n\nhas the complementary solution ${y}_{c}=A{e}^{-5x},$ solve the initial value problem.\n\n$y(x)=$\n{{field-1}}" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040400/state.json" "file_sha256" "00c2f57a1e14ce294f3828d1cea1966c3408c0d0f3ac5366645345a6a40072e1"} {"question" "q-332782" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "To find the general solution of an inhomogeneous first-order linear equation, we must find the sum of the complementary and particular solutions.\n\nTo find the complementary solution ${y}_{c},$ we solve the corresponding homogeneous equation, given by\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}^{′}+5{y}_{c}=0.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation, we find that the complementary solution is\n\n$$\n{y}_{c}=A{e}^{-5x},\n$$\n\nwhere $A$ is an arbitrary constant.\n\nWe now need to find the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x).$\n\nThe right-hand side of the inhomogeneous equation is a constant. Therefore, we assume that the particular integral is also a constant, i.e.,\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=\\beta ,\n$$\n\nwhere $\\beta $ is a constant to be determined.\n\nCalculating the first derivative of ${y}_{p}$ gives\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}{y}_{p}}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nTo find the value of $\\beta ,$ we substitute ${y}_{p}$ and ${y}_{p}^{′}$ into the differential equation:\n\n$$\n0+5\\beta =10\n$$\n\nSolving for $\\beta $ gives $\\beta =2.$\n\nTherefore, the particular solution ${y}_{p}(x)$ is\n\n$$\n{y}_{p}(x)=2.\n$$\n\nThe general solution is given by\n\n$$\ny(x)={y}_{c}(x)+{y}_{p}(x),\n$$\n\nand therefore, the general solution in our case is\n\n$$\ny=A{e}^{-5x}+2.\n$$\n\nNow, we can find the constant $A$ using the initial conditions.\n\nSubstituting $y(0)=1$ into the general solution gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{e}^{0}+2 & =1 \\\\ A+2 & =1 \\\\ A & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution to the initial value problem is\n\n$$\ny=-{e}^{-5x}+2.\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040400/state.json" "file_sha256" "00c2f57a1e14ce294f3828d1cea1966c3408c0d0f3ac5366645345a6a40072e1"} {"question" "q-332782" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040400/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "8c7a8ddd558282989143d3a80d5d2f442156b5a3285ad5a583c66ebbc09d55c4"} {"question" "q-332782" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/blank "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040400/state.json" "file_sha256" "00c2f57a1e14ce294f3828d1cea1966c3408c0d0f3ac5366645345a6a40072e1"} {"question" "q-332782" "field" "field-1" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2-e^{-5x}"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/differential/14040400/state.json" "file_sha256" "00c2f57a1e14ce294f3828d1cea1966c3408c0d0f3ac5366645345a6a40072e1"}] "usage" {} "basis" 1451 "retractions" [] "immutable_answers" [] "immutable_fields" [] "no_history" []}
